  "slug": "ai-flows-are-strong-but-narrow",
  "draft": {
    "title": "AI Flows Are Strong but Narrow",
    "description": "Vanda sees selective retail AI demand as institutional buying slows. Our measurements show strong AI participation but weak breadth and caution.",
    "slug": "ai-flows-are-strong-but-narrow",
    "body_html": "<p class=\"press-byline\">Research Desk — Mastermind Research</p><p>Vanda Research’s <a href=\"https://www.mastermind-x.com/research/vanda-research-what-equity-flows-are-telling-us-under-the-hood-16sep20-53a7ed.html\">September 21 coverage</a> makes a clean distinction: retail demand remains active in selected AI names while institutional buying has lost pace. The 4-page report argues that the market is not seeing a broad, indiscriminate rush into the theme. It instead sees a concentrated search for particular exposures, with the next broad index move dependent on easier policy conditions and less geopolitical strain.</p><h2>What the desk argued</h2><p>Vanda’s central observation is that institutional call activity in the Mag 7 and SOXX-linked AI complex stalled after bullish positioning had already built. That matters because a slowdown in incremental buying can leave a popular theme dependent on a narrower set of participants. The report does not call the AI trade abandoned; it describes a change in who is supplying the marginal demand.</p><p>Retail activity provides the counterweight. CRWD represented 53% of retail call activity, with 1M calls at the 97th percentile, according to the desk’s reading. NVDA accounted for 63% of SOXX retail inflows, while 19/30 semiconductor names showed positive buying. That combination supports Vanda’s claim that retail interest is selective rather than simply chasing every name attached to the theme.</p><h2>What our data says</h2><p>Our measurements agree that the market is split. Mastermind’s market-state composite was 53 of 100, or MIXED, on 2026-10-09. Trend and technicals scored 70, but risk appetite scored 27 and breadth and participation scored 0. Those readings fit a market where price action can remain resilient in favored groups without demonstrating broad underlying participation.</p><p>The breadth data is especially consistent with Vanda’s focus on concentration. As of 2026-10-08, 62.3% of the AI cohort was above its 50-day average, compared with 27.0% of the non-AI cohort. The 35.3 point spread says leadership is real, but it also says the leadership has not spread evenly through the rest of the market.</p><p>Dealer positioning adds a second qualification. SPX dealer gamma was 76.9bn net, while SPY dealer gamma was 1.59bn net and spot sat 0.15% from its flip level. QQQ dealer gamma was 0.92bn net, with spot 0.24% from its flip level. These are conditions in which index behavior may be sensitive to relatively small changes around key levels, even as stock-specific flows continue.</p><h2>What would change the picture</h2><p>A broader improvement in participation would weaken the case that this is chiefly a concentrated AI-flow story. Our breadth measure is currently the clearest point of disagreement with a more durable, market-wide advance: it stands at 0, despite the 70 score for trend and technicals.</p><p>The risk backdrop also argues for observation over extrapolation. The risk radar remained in caution on 2026-10-09, with rates the dominant scare at 70.2 and modelled 21-session drawdown probability at 0.19. The fear/greed dial was 52, or Neutral, across 11 qualifying legs. A turn toward broader participation, alongside calmer rate pressure and sustained institutional buying, would materially alter this reading. Until then, the evidence supports watching selective AI demand rather than treating it as proof of a fully broad market move.</p><p class=\"press-footer\">Educational content — not investment advice. Markets involve risk.</p>"
  },
